course: dictionary
lesson: 15217
title: The flower's green protectors
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sepal is a small part of a flower that sits under the petals. It is usually green and looks a bit like a leaf.
explain: Its job is to protect the flower bud before it opens. «The green sepals wrapped tightly around the bud.» The bud stays safe inside them until it is ready to open.
explain: In science lessons you label flower parts: petal, sepal, stamen and stigma. Petals are often bright and colourful, but sepals are usually green. «Petals and sepals» are two different parts, so look underneath to find the sepals.
fact: A sepal is one of the small, usually green, leaf-like parts under the petals of a flower.
fact: Sepals protect the bud before it opens.
fact: «Sepal» is a noun. One is a sepal, and more than one are sepals.
fact: «Petals and sepals» are two different flower parts, not the same thing.
fact: Context clues like green, protect and bud can help you work out what a sepal does.
exercise: «The green sepals wrapped tightly around the bud.» What do the sepals protect? | The roots underground | *The bud before it opens | The bees that visit the flower | The petals after they fall
exercise: Which part of a flower is usually green and sits under the petals? | *sepal | stamen | stigma | petal
exercise: Mia is labelling a flower in science: petal, sepal, stamen, stigma. Which label is the part that protects the bud? | stigma | petal | *sepal | stamen
test: «Remove the petals and you will see the sepals underneath.» Where are the sepals found? | Above the petals | Inside the stamen | *Under the petals | In the roots
test: What is a sepal? | A bright colour on the petals | A tiny insect that visits flowers | A type of root | *A small, usually green, leaf-like part under the petals
exam: Which statement about a sepal is correct? | It is a bright, colourful part on top of the petals | It is a root that grows under the soil | *It is a small, usually green, leaf-like part that protects the bud | It is the same thing as a stamen
